---
import Layout from "../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import ComponentDocs from "../../../components/ComponentDocs.astro";
---

<Layout title="hp-layout">
  <ComponentDocs title="hp-layout">
    <p slot="description">
      Hex layout primitive — what <code>display: flex</code> is for rows, this is for the
      axial lattice. Slotted children carrying <code>q</code> / <code>r</code> attributes
      are placed on the lattice, and the element sizes to the content it placed, so it sits
      in normal document flow and the page scrolls it. There is no camera: no pan, no zoom,
      no inertia. Reach for <a href="/hexpunk/components/layout/hp-grid">
      <code>&lt;hp-grid&gt;</code></a> when the surface should behave like a viewport
      instead.
    </p>

    <hp-demo caption="free — children keep their authored q / r">
      <hp-layout>
        <hp-cell variant="anchor" q="0" r="0">0,0</hp-cell>
        <hp-cell variant="action" q="1" r="0">1,0</hp-cell>
        <hp-cell variant="secondary" q="0" r="1">0,1</hp-cell>
        <hp-cell variant="utility" q="-1" r="1">-1,1</hp-cell>
      </hp-layout>

      <hp-code slot="code" language="html">{`<body>
  <hp-layout>
    <hp-cell variant="anchor"    q="0"  r="0">0,0</hp-cell>
    <hp-cell variant="action"    q="1"  r="0">1,0</hp-cell>
    <hp-cell variant="secondary" q="0"  r="1">0,1</hp-cell>
    <hp-cell variant="utility"   q="-1" r="1">-1,1</hp-cell>
  </hp-layout>
</body>`}</hp-code>
    </hp-demo>

    <h2>Sizing to content</h2>
    <p>
      The element measures the hexes it placed and reports that size, so surrounding
      content flows around it exactly as it would around a <code>div</code>. Nothing is
      clipped and nothing needs an explicit height — the two paragraphs below are ordinary
      siblings.
    </p>

    <hp-demo caption="In flow — no fixed height, no clipping">
      <p>Text above the layout.</p>
      <hp-layout>
        <hp-cell variant="content" q="0" r="0" size="sm">a</hp-cell>
        <hp-cell variant="content" q="1" r="0" size="sm">b</hp-cell>
        <hp-cell variant="content" q="2" r="0" size="sm">c</hp-cell>
      </hp-layout>
      <p>Text below it, pushed down by the measured height.</p>

      <hp-code slot="code" language="html">{`<body>
  <p>Text above the layout.</p>

  <!-- No height needed: the element reports the content's size
       through --hp-layout-width / --hp-layout-height. -->
  <hp-layout>
    <hp-cell variant="content" q="0" r="0" size="sm">a</hp-cell>
    <hp-cell variant="content" q="1" r="0" size="sm">b</hp-cell>
    <hp-cell variant="content" q="2" r="0" size="sm">c</hp-cell>
  </hp-layout>

  <p>Text below it, pushed down by the measured height.</p>
</body>`}</hp-code>
    </hp-demo>

    <h2>Packing</h2>
    <p>
      <code>spiral</code> and <code>rows</code> run the same first-fit-decreasing packer
      <code>&lt;hp-grid&gt;</code> uses — largest shape first, then the first free position
      the scan offers. Children publish the cells they occupy via
      <code>data-fill-cells</code> (<code>&lt;hp-cluster&gt;</code> does this
      automatically); anything without it counts as a single hex.
    </p>

    <hp-demo caption="spiral — rings outward from the origin">
      <hp-layout layout="spiral">
        <hp-cell variant="anchor" filled>1</hp-cell>
        <hp-cell variant="action">2</hp-cell>
        <hp-cell variant="action">3</hp-cell>
        <hp-cell variant="secondary">4</hp-cell>
        <hp-cell variant="secondary">5</hp-cell>
        <hp-cell variant="utility">6</hp-cell>
        <hp-cell variant="utility">7</hp-cell>
      </hp-layout>

      <hp-code slot="code" language="html">{`<body>
  <!-- q / r are assigned by the packer; authored values are ignored. -->
  <hp-layout layout="spiral">
    <hp-cell variant="anchor" filled>1</hp-cell>
    <hp-cell variant="action">2</hp-cell>
    <hp-cell variant="action">3</hp-cell>
  </hp-layout>
</body>`}</hp-code>
    </hp-demo>

    <hp-demo caption="rows — reading order, wrapping at a width cap">
      <hp-layout layout="rows">
        <hp-cell variant="anchor" filled>1</hp-cell>
        <hp-cell variant="action">2</hp-cell>
        <hp-cell variant="action">3</hp-cell>
        <hp-cell variant="secondary">4</hp-cell>
        <hp-cell variant="secondary">5</hp-cell>
        <hp-cell variant="utility">6</hp-cell>
      </hp-layout>

      <hp-code slot="code" language="html">{`<body>
  <hp-layout layout="rows">
    <hp-cell variant="anchor" filled>1</hp-cell>
    <hp-cell variant="action">2</hp-cell>
    <hp-cell variant="action">3</hp-cell>
  </hp-layout>
</body>`}</hp-code>
    </hp-demo>

    <h2>Dragging</h2>
    <p>
      <code>draggable</code> opts the surface into drag-to-move. A cell dropped on an
      occupied neighbour snaps to the nearest free one — the same nearest-free search
      <code>&lt;hp-grid&gt;</code> runs, so a given drag lands in the same place on either
      surface. Individual cells opt out with <code>draggable="false"</code>. Because there
      is no camera, dragging empty space does nothing and the page keeps its scroll.
    </p>

    <hp-demo caption="draggable — drag a cell; PIN refuses">
      <hp-layout draggable>
        <hp-cell variant="anchor" q="0" r="0">drag</hp-cell>
        <hp-cell variant="action" q="1" r="0">drag</hp-cell>
        <hp-cell variant="utility" q="0" r="1" draggable="false">pin</hp-cell>
      </hp-layout>

      <hp-code slot="code" language="html">{`<body>
  <hp-layout draggable>
    <hp-cell variant="anchor" q="0" r="0">drag</hp-cell>
    <hp-cell variant="action" q="1" r="0">drag</hp-cell>

    <!-- Opts this one cell out of an otherwise draggable surface. -->
    <hp-cell variant="utility" q="0" r="1" draggable="false">pin</hp-cell>
  </hp-layout>

  <script type="module">
    document.querySelector("hp-layout").addEventListener("hp-layout-drop", (event) => {
      console.log(event.detail.element, "settled at", event.detail.at);
    });
  <\/script>
</body>`}</hp-code>
    </hp-demo>

    <Fragment slot="intent">
      <h2>hp-layout or hp-grid?</h2>
      <p>
        Both place hexes on the same lattice and share the same placement code, so they
        never disagree about where a cell belongs. They differ in what surrounds that
        placement.
      </p>
      <hp-banner tone="do">
        Use <code>&lt;hp-layout&gt;</code> when the hexes are part of a page: a toolbar, a
        set of status cells, a small navigation cluster, anything you would otherwise reach
        for flexbox to arrange. It costs nothing beyond the element itself.
      </hp-banner>
      <hp-banner tone="do">
        Use <code>&lt;hp-grid&gt;</code> when the hexes are a world you move through —
        camera pan and zoom, a rendered hex field, content that reveals itself as cells
        grow, diving into a cell, tethers between cells. That surface is canvas-rendered
        and carries a rendering dependency, so it is a deliberate choice rather than a
        default.
      </hp-banner>
      <hp-banner tone="dont">
        Don't wrap <code>&lt;hp-layout&gt;</code> in a fixed-height box and expect to pan
        inside it. It has no camera by design; a scrollable region should scroll, and a
        pannable one should be an <code>&lt;hp-grid&gt;</code>.
      </hp-banner>

      <h2>Accessibility</h2>
      <p>
        The element only positions its children — it adds no roles and takes no focus, so
        the accessible structure is whatever you slot into it. Cells that do something
        should be focusable in their own right. Placement is visual only: the DOM order you
        author is the order assistive technology and keyboard navigation follow, which is
        worth keeping deliberate when a packer decides the visual arrangement.
      </p>
      <p>
        Drag-to-move is pointer-only today; a keyboard alternative is still outstanding, so
        don't make a drag the only way to complete a task.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
  </ComponentDocs>
</Layout>
